                        ],
                        "form": "mogged"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "597244fa-162d-4fc9-a80a-b1003244924a",
                        "entry_id": "0766f99b-89ce-361e-b058-4edc971f115e",
                        "tags": [
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "mogged"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                        "entry_id": "0766f99b-89ce-361e-b058-4edc971f115e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To move or walk slowly; to trudge, amble; (more broadly) to go."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "US",
                            "dialectal",
                            "intransitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d8c3488-4dc8-4a58-b15a-41bd07172d84",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "to mog about"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ee408a82-b4d9-4e09-bb79-0d0f1d57ad00",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "to mog off"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "46ea680f-b719-4d6f-b9c4-3bacc880b1ea",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "They marched, grumbling and threatening, but Sile never paid a bit of attention. He just mogged on behind, and every time one of them turned, he wiggled his gun, and that was enough."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6e40b12b-68ff-4299-b92c-b31d6396e28a",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "He was mogging around on the Muskrat Creek near Indian Lake when he shot and wounded a big buck with the finest head that he had ever seen, a set of horns that he would never forget."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "655b5c61-a77b-4d42-b1e3-88a713e83a37",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "But mostly, that dark and rainy afternoon — as I mogged back and forth between boat and office, and then, all books and papers and twenty-seven boxes of Nick the Nose safely aboard, distributed the cargo here and there from stem to stanchion— I thought about Gaybee, her place in the ground, and how sorrowfully soon she had come to it."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8b5b346e-82f9-4808-87ed-94326fe67ff4",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "\"Thirty pounds a day,\" Alex said. \"Mogging about in the jungle is unhealthy—fever, tigers, and all that.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f64058be-82e4-4bce-ad24-7f7016bf8ecc",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "Two girls hugged her and they all put on boots and shoes and mogged away to the wagons."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d4fe3c36-22e9-43c3-aa69-ad4e1e4e2ada",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "I had to do everything on my own. But I made the best of it and mogged on."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "38789fb9-76c7-4923-84d4-104da488ca13",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "We know that economist and ex-con Vicky Pryce went properly nuclear when her husband Chris Huhne mogged off with his aide, resulting in wrecked political careers, a family torn asunder and custodial sentences."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c978c75f-f402-4957-a8a8-5e73c1b9d87d",
                                "sense_id": "e71393de-8f24-39a6-abaf-2d623f71da00",
                                "text": "They're loyal. And you're not one of us, so we want you out. We're taking over this business. And tha can mog off right now."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1a2bfa28-e6ab-3fd8-b777-cfbe4ca1d209",
                        "entry_id": "0766f99b-89ce-361e-b058-4edc971f115e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To cause to move; to drive."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "US",
                            "dialectal",
                            "rare",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fb071f2d-28cd-4061-ae21-41477a52af91",
                                "sense_id": "1a2bfa28-e6ab-3fd8-b777-cfbe4ca1d209",
                                "text": "'Tell John to mog the cows i' in the mornin'—it's time as they wenten i' the Cote Leasnow.'"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6b441c0e-3281-4bf5-a04f-a0d9f760dc67",
                                "sense_id": "1a2bfa28-e6ab-3fd8-b777-cfbe4ca1d209",
                                "text": "\"I've been directed here to find Miss Susan Pike,\" the man outside explained, between fresh coughings. ¶ \"Well, then, mog your boots out of this as quick as ever you can!\" my Aunt replied, with great promptitude. \"You won't find her here!\""
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5f418550-48a9-31c1-91e7-41764a1a3c02",
                        "entry_id": "0766f99b-89ce-361e-b058-4edc971f115e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "In the game of costly colours: to exchange (a card) with the dealer."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "intransitive",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9c9241c2-4ef4-4d93-b333-8d5f18fe8db5",
                                "sense_id": "5f418550-48a9-31c1-91e7-41764a1a3c02",
                                "text": "Mogging or Changing. The cards being dealt, the first player asks the dealer if he will mog, i. e. exchange a card with him. Should he refuse, the challenger scores 1. […] If the opposite partners do not mog, the dealer has the privilege of mogging with the 'deck,' which he does by taking the card next under the trump and substituting in its place one out of his own hand."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "1902eb08-9fc9-37b5-bc20-32347c4a3960",
